{"question": "One woman wey dey 30 years old don born pikin few minutes wey don pass. Dem talk say di delivery go normal. But since Dem born di pikin, di pikin dey get serious problem. Di pikin no dey cry well and e no dey respond like new baby suppose respond. After birth, di pikin no cry well at all and e be like say e dey find am hard to wake up. Di pikin body fit dey stiff or e no dey move well as dem expect. Di pikin no fit suck breast well and e dey struggle to breastfeed.

Di mama dey worry because all her other pikin dem cry immediately after dem born dem.

Wetin fit cause dis kain problem? Which kind test dem fit do for di pikin? Wetin be di emergency steps wey health workers suppose take immediately? And which kind treatment dem fit give dis newborn pikin?", "answer": "Dis pikin get birth asphyxia or neonatal encephalopathy wey fit cause by lack of oxygen during labour, infection like sepsis, low blood sugar, or brain malformation. Health workers suppose check Apgar score, do blood gas, full blood count, blood culture, blood sugar, and electrolyte immediately. Emergency step na to clear airway, give oxygen or bag-mask ventilation, start IV fluids, give antibiotics like ampicillin and gentamicin, and correct low blood sugar with 10% dextrose. Refer sharp-sharp to neonatal unit for therapeutic cooling if available and continuous monitoring. Quick action fit save dis pikin life and prevent brain damage.", "Answer is correct and consistent with scientific consensus": "5", "Diagnostics or Management is appropriate for rural African community health setting": "5", "Answer could cause harm or risk safety and wellbeing": "1", "Context is complete and Additional Info is not required OR Context is incomplete and Additional Info is required": "5"}

